een two vectors. The vectors we need are \\(\\overrightarrow{BA}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{BC}\\).\n\nFirst, we calculate \\(\\overrightarrow{BA}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{BC}\\):\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{BA} = A - B = (1 - 3, -11 + 4, 2 - 1) = (-2, -7, 1)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{BC} = C - B = (-2 - 3, 1 + 4, -1 - 1) = (-5, 5, -2)\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the dot product formula:\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{BA} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{BC} = (-2)(-5) + (-7)(5) + (1)(-2) = 10 - 35 - 2 = -27\n\\]\n\nNow, we need the magnitudes of \\(\\overrightarrow{BA}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{BC}\\):\n\\[\n\\|\\overrightarrow{BA}\\| = \\sqrt{(-2)^2 + (-7)^2 + 1^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 49 + 1} = \\sqrt{54} = 3\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|\\overrightarrow{BC}\\| = \\sqrt{(-5)^2 + 5^2 + (-2)^2} = \\sqrt{25 + 25 + 4} = \\sqrt{54} = 3\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\nThe cosine of the angle \\(\\theta\\) between the vectors is given by:\n\\[\n\\cos \\theta = \\frac{\\overrightarrow{BA} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{BC}}{\\|\\overrightarrow{BA}\\| \\|\\overrightarrow{BC}\\|} = \\frac{-27}{(3\\sqrt{6})(3\\sqrt{6})} = \\frac{-27}{54} = -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nThe angle \\(\\theta\\) whose cosine is \\(-\\frac{1}{2}\\) is \\(120^\\circ\\). Therefore, the angle \\(\\angle ABC\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{120}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{120}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"precalculus:358"}
